1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
This tube of iron thiocyanate has sat at

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:04,920
the same pale red for an hour.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,440
Is that proof that it's an equilibrium, with

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:08,960
both reactions still running?

5
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:10,640
Not on its own.

6
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,360
A finished reaction looks still, too.

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,280
To show that a reaction can run both

8
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,920
ways, and that it responds to a change,

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,520
you have to push it and watch what

10
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:20,800
happens.

11
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:23,280
So how do you plan an investigation with

12
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:24,640
evidence you can trust?

13
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:27,786
Here's the test for reversibility.

14
00:00:28,427 --> 00:00:30,986
Change the conditions so the reaction runs one

15
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:33,866
way, then reverse the change and see whether

16
00:00:33,947 --> 00:00:35,786
the starting substance comes back.

17
00:00:36,507 --> 00:00:39,066
Heat pink cobalt chloride crystals in a dry

18
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:39,866
test tube.

19
00:00:40,347 --> 00:00:43,306
Water leaves the crystals as steam, droplets collect

20
00:00:43,387 --> 00:00:45,706
near the top, and the solid turns blue.

21
00:00:46,347 --> 00:00:48,506
Going one way proves nothing yet.

22
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:50,986
Now add a few drops of water to

23
00:00:51,027 --> 00:00:52,346
the cooled blue solid.

24
00:00:52,747 --> 00:00:55,066
It turns pink again, and the tube warms

25
00:00:55,147 --> 00:00:55,706
slightly.

26
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,466
It went there and came back, so the

27
00:00:58,507 --> 00:01:00,186
reaction is reversible.

28
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,160
Compare burning magnesium.

29
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,960
It burns with a blinding white flame and

30
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,200
leaves white magnesium oxide powder.

31
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:11,880
Add water, heat it, cool it: nothing you

32
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:13,800
can do in a school lab brings shiny

33
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:14,840
magnesium back.

34
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,760
So it's irreversible.

35
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,240
And notice that the cobalt test ran in

36
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:20,360
an open tube.

37
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:22,600
The steam escaped, so we had to add

38
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:23,400
water back.

39
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:26,760
That's fine for showing reversibility, but it isn't

40
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:27,880
an equilibrium.

41
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:32,060
To study an equilibrium, nothing that takes part

42
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:32,860
can escape.

43
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:35,900
In a tube of dissolved ions, the ions

44
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:38,460
stay in the solution, and a gas mixture

45
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:41,020
needs a sealed tube or syringe.

46
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,660
So write the condition into your method: what's

47
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,460
sealed, and what could get in or out.

48
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:49,340
That matters, because a response to a disturbance

49
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,980
only makes sense if the system is closed.

50
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,400
Now the disturbance investigation.

51
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:58,640
Mix equal volumes of dilute iron three nitrate

52
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,760
and potassium thiocyanate to get a pale red

53
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:02,520
stock.

54
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,120
Pale is deliberate: you need to see a

55
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,920
change in either direction.

56
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,240
Pour equal amounts into six identical tubes.

57
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,960
Tube one is the reference, and you never

58
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:13,520
touch it.

59
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,560
Every other tube gets one change, and you

60
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:19,120
judge every colour side by side with tube

61
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,000
one against a white tile, because colour from

62
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:23,760
memory is unreliable.

63
00:02:24,967 --> 00:02:26,486
So, the variables.

64
00:02:26,767 --> 00:02:30,326
The independent variable is what you change: which

65
00:02:30,407 --> 00:02:32,406
substance you add, or the temperature.

66
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:35,766
The dependent variable is what you observe: the

67
00:02:35,847 --> 00:02:37,686
colour, compared with the reference.

68
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:40,726
The controlled variables are everything you keep the

69
00:02:40,807 --> 00:02:43,606
same: the volume of stock in each tube,

70
00:02:43,647 --> 00:02:46,566
the tube size, the background and lighting, and

71
00:02:46,647 --> 00:02:48,566
how long you wait before judging.

72
00:02:49,767 --> 00:02:52,166
Here's what to expect, and why, from the

73
00:02:52,287 --> 00:02:53,366
disturbance videos.

74
00:02:53,927 --> 00:02:56,006
Add a few crystals of iron nitrate to

75
00:02:56,087 --> 00:02:59,766
tube two, or potassium thiocyanate to tube three.

76
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:03,206
Adding a reactant shifts the equilibrium right, so

77
00:03:03,287 --> 00:03:04,566
the red deepens.

78
00:03:05,127 --> 00:03:07,926
Add sodium hydroxide drop by drop to tube

79
00:03:08,087 --> 00:03:08,886
four.

80
00:03:08,927 --> 00:03:11,206
It removes iron three plus ions as a

81
00:03:11,287 --> 00:03:15,206
faint rust-brown solid, so the equilibrium shifts left,

82
00:03:15,247 --> 00:03:16,646
and the red fades.

83
00:03:17,867 --> 00:03:18,506
But wait.

84
00:03:18,747 --> 00:03:22,266
Those drops of sodium hydroxide solution also added

85
00:03:22,347 --> 00:03:22,826
water.

86
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:25,786
A paler tube could simply be more dilute.

87
00:03:26,347 --> 00:03:29,466
So tube five is the dilution control: add

88
00:03:29,547 --> 00:03:31,866
the same number of drops of plain water.

89
00:03:32,347 --> 00:03:34,546
It gets only a tiny bit paler.

90
00:03:34,907 --> 00:03:37,306
If tube four is clearly paler than tube

91
00:03:37,387 --> 00:03:40,266
five, the fading is a real shift, not

92
00:03:40,347 --> 00:03:41,466
just dilution.

93
00:03:41,787 --> 00:03:44,266
Adding solids to tubes two and three avoided

94
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:46,826
this, because the volume hardly changed.

95
00:03:48,034 --> 00:03:51,793
For temperature, use the cobalt solution: pink cobalt

96
00:03:51,874 --> 00:03:54,433
ions with lots of chloride, which form a

97
00:03:54,514 --> 00:03:56,593
blue ion in a reaction that takes in

98
00:03:56,754 --> 00:03:57,233
heat.

99
00:03:57,554 --> 00:03:59,793
Stopper the tube, because water is part of

100
00:03:59,874 --> 00:04:01,953
this reaction, so none should escape.

101
00:04:02,594 --> 00:04:04,433
In a hot water bath at about sixty

102
00:04:04,514 --> 00:04:06,193
degrees, it turns blue.

103
00:04:06,674 --> 00:04:08,753
In an ice bath, it turns pink.

104
00:04:09,314 --> 00:04:12,353
Back at room temperature, the starting purple returns,

105
00:04:12,434 --> 00:04:14,113
so it's reversible too.

106
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:18,213
Three words exam questions love.

107
00:04:18,774 --> 00:04:21,573
Validity: does the test really measure what it

108
00:04:21,654 --> 00:04:22,373
claims to?

109
00:04:22,854 --> 00:04:25,493
The reference tube, one change per tube and

110
00:04:25,534 --> 00:04:28,453
the dilution control make your conclusion valid.

111
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:31,733
Reliability: do you get the same result when

112
00:04:31,774 --> 00:04:32,533
you repeat it?

113
00:04:32,934 --> 00:04:35,653
Repeat each tube, compare with other groups, and

114
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:37,893
have two people judge the colours.

115
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:40,933
Accuracy: how close is your result to the

116
00:04:41,014 --> 00:04:41,813
true value?

117
00:04:42,214 --> 00:04:44,293
Judging by eye is only rough.

118
00:04:44,614 --> 00:04:46,933
A colourimeter gives a number, and you'll see

119
00:04:47,054 --> 00:04:48,293
that later in the module.

120
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:51,900
Before any of this, you write a risk

121
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:55,180
assessment: each hazard, the harm it could do,

122
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:56,780
and how you'll control it.

123
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:00,140
Cobalt chloride is harmful if swallowed, may cause

124
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:02,540
cancer if its dust is breathed in, and

125
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:05,900
can cause allergies, so use small amounts, avoid

126
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,140
making dust, and wear gloves.

127
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:11,420
Never let thiocyanate touch an acid, because that

128
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:13,700
can release a very toxic gas.

129
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:17,580
Sodium hydroxide, even dilute, can harm your eyes,

130
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:19,500
so wear safety glasses.

131
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:21,660
The hot bath can scald, so use a

132
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,100
test tube holder.

133
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:25,820
Never look straight at burning magnesium.

134
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:27,740
And all the waste goes into a labelled

135
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:29,900
container, never down the sink.

136
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:32,220
Two lines to keep.

137
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:35,660
Push it, then bring it back: one change

138
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:39,500
proves nothing, but a return proves it's reversible.

139
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:42,620
And for cobalt chloride: pink in the rain,

140
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,100
blue in the desert.

141
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:46,940
Water makes it pink, and drying or heating

142
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:47,980
makes it blue.

143
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:49,840
Your turn.

144
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:53,040
A student adds ten drops of silver nitrate

145
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:56,160
solution to an iron thiocyanate tube, and it

146
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:58,240
turns paler than the reference.

147
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:01,640
She concludes that the equilibrium shifted left.

148
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,960
What extra tube would make this conclusion valid,

149
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:06,080
and why?

150
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:07,440
Pause here to answer.

151
00:06:17,867 --> 00:06:19,706
Add the same ten drops of water to

152
00:06:19,787 --> 00:06:22,826
an identical tube: a dilution control.

153
00:06:23,147 --> 00:06:26,426
The silver nitrate solution added volume, so some

154
00:06:26,467 --> 00:06:28,706
of the paling could just be dilution.

155
00:06:28,987 --> 00:06:31,626
If the silver nitrate tube is clearly paler

156
00:06:31,707 --> 00:06:34,186
than the water tube, the shift is real.

157
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:37,146
Repeating the same tube doesn't fix this.

158
00:06:37,547 --> 00:06:40,106
It makes the result more reliable, not more

159
00:06:40,187 --> 00:06:40,746
valid.

160
00:06:41,967 --> 00:06:42,806
One more.

161
00:06:43,087 --> 00:06:46,046
A student heats pink cobalt chloride crystals in

162
00:06:46,127 --> 00:06:48,366
an open test tube, and they turn blue.

163
00:06:48,927 --> 00:06:52,686
He writes, "This shows the reaction is reversible."

164
00:06:52,967 --> 00:06:54,126
Evaluate his conclusion.

165
00:06:54,687 --> 00:06:55,966
Pause here to answer.

166
00:07:06,367 --> 00:07:08,046
It isn't supported yet.

167
00:07:08,287 --> 00:07:10,366
Heating shows only one direction.

168
00:07:10,767 --> 00:07:13,326
To show reversibility, he has to reverse the

169
00:07:13,407 --> 00:07:16,286
change: add water to the cooled blue solid

170
00:07:16,527 --> 00:07:17,966
and see it turn pink again.

171
00:07:18,367 --> 00:07:20,006
And because the tube was open and the

172
00:07:20,047 --> 00:07:24,526
water escaped, this tests reversibility, not an equilibrium.

173
00:07:24,927 --> 00:07:28,206
If you wrote "correct, because the colour changed",

174
00:07:28,407 --> 00:07:31,086
remember that one change isn't a return.

175
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:35,980
So, to plan an equilibrium investigation: push the

176
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:38,700
reaction, then bring it back, to show it's

177
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:39,740
reversible.

178
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:43,820
Use a closed system, keep an untouched reference,

179
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:46,700
make one change per tube, add a dilution

180
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:49,660
control, and judge colours side by side.

181
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:52,060
Write the risk assessment first.

182
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:55,900
Next: putting a number on where an equilibrium

183
00:07:55,980 --> 00:07:56,620
sits.

184
00:07:57,834 --> 00:07:59,273
Here are the key notes.

185
00:07:59,514 --> 00:08:01,433
Pause the video if you need more time

186
00:08:01,514 --> 00:08:02,313
to copy them.
